          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 181,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When you crack your knuckles, the distinct popping or cracking sound comes from **gas bubbles forming and reacting to pressure changes inside the fluid that lubricates your joints**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 270,
                "end_index": 485,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Anatomy of the Joint\nYour finger joints (specifically the metacarpophalangeal joints, where your fingers meet your hand) are surrounded by a capsule filled with a thick, clear substance called **synovial fluid**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 487,
                "end_index": 571,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This fluid acts like a lubricant, helping your bones glide smoothly past one another"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 573,
                "end_index": 680,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Naturally dissolved within this fluid are various gases, primarily **nitrogen, carbon dioxide, and oxygen**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 690,
                "end_index": 863,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Physics of the \"Crack\"\nWhen you bend, pull, or press your fingers to crack them, you are physically stretching the joint capsule and pulling the two bones slightly apart"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 865,
                "end_index": 1010,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This triggers a chain reaction:\n* **The Pressure Drops:** Pulling the bones apart suddenly increases the volume of space inside the joint capsule"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1012,
                "end_index": 1130,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because the capsule is sealed, this increase in volume causes a rapid drop in pressure (creating a vacuum-like effect)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1132,
                "end_index": 1304,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Cavitation (Bubble Formation):** Because of the sudden drop in pressure, the gases dissolved in the synovial fluid rapidly come out of the solution to form a gas bubble"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1306,
                "end_index": 1354,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This process is known in physics as *cavitation*"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1522,
                "end_index": 1597,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* In **2015**, researchers used real-time MRI video to watch knuckles crack"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1599,
                "end_index": 1775,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "They observed that the sound occurred the exact moment the gas bubble **formed** (a process called *tribonucleation*), and noted that the bubble remained in the joint afterward"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1777,
                "end_index": 1940,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* In **2018**, another team used advanced mathematical modeling to show that the sound is actually caused by the **partial collapse** of these newly formed bubbles"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1942,
                "end_index": 2103,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The rapid, microscopic collapse of even a portion of the bubble is enough to produce the acoustic sound wave we hear, while still leaving a visible bubble behind"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2106,
                "end_index": 2221,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "In short, the sound is the acoustic signature of a gas bubble rapidly forming and shifting inside the joint's fluid"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2288,
                "end_index": 2392,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Once you crack a knuckle, you usually have to wait about **15 to 30 minutes** before you can do it again"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2394,
                "end_index": 2508,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This \"refractory period\" is the time it takes for the gas bubble to fully re-dissolve back into the synovial fluid"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2510,
                "end_index": 2636,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Until the gas is completely dissolved back into the liquid, you cannot create the drop in pressure needed to form a new bubble"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2808,
                "end_index": 2819,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It could be"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2808,
                "end_index": 2968,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It could be:\n* **Moving tendons or ligaments:** As a joint moves, a tendon can slide slightly out of place and make a snapping sound as it pops back over a bone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2970,
                "end_index": 3095,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Rough surfaces:** In arthritic joints, the loss of smooth cartilage can cause bones to grind or click against one another"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3146,
                "end_index": 3218,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "** There is no scientific evidence linking knuckle cracking to arthritis"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3222,
                "end_index": 3301,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The most famous proof of this comes from a California doctor named Donald Unger"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3303,
                "end_index": 3471,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To test the theory (and prove his mother wrong), he cracked the knuckles on his **left hand** at least twice a day for over 50 years, while leaving his right hand alone"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3473,
                "end_index": 3661,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "After half a century, he examined both hands and found absolutely no difference between them and no signs of arthritis in either hand\u2014an experiment that won him an Ig Nobel Prize in 2009"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3665,
                "end_index": 3840,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "*(Note: While it won't cause arthritis, habitual and extremely forceful knuckle cracking has occasionally been linked to minor hand swelling or reduced grip strength over time"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
